<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Caithness</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Caithness is a historic county in the far north-east of mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, bounded by the Pentland Firth and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>. It is rich in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> brochs and was part of Pictish territory before Norse settlers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a> took it over in the 9th century; the earls of Orkney later held it as a Scottish earldom. Its towns include Wick and Thurso, and its coast ends at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duncansby-head">Duncansby Head</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunnet-head">Dunnet Head</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/caithness.jpg" alt="Caithness" width="320" height="482" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User:Visitor from Wikishire. This particular view by User:Inops · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caithness-Scotland.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
